#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172879} :activity/id #uuid "9e60f656-c2ee-5768-b1af-a3eb8499887f" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049387 :topic/id #uuid "f0b7f9f5-b45c-5478-b9c9-cc76a52e64dd" :topic/math-academy-id 3530} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172879 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173151 :tutorial/content "When finding the roots of a transformed radical function, we need to consider whether the radical's index is odd or even.\n\nLet's start with the odd case first. As an example, we will find the root of the function $f(x)$, given by\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\sqrt[{\\color{blue}{3}}]{10-4x} + 2\\,\n$$\n\nNotice that we're dealing with a cube root, and so the index of the radical is ${\\color{blue}{3}}$, which is odd.\n\nTo find the root of $f(x)$, we must solve\n\n$$\nf(x)=0\n$$\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[3]{10 - 4x} + 2 = 0\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation by making $x$ the subject, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt[3]{10 - 4x} + 2 &= 0 \\\\\n\\sqrt[3]{10 - 4x} &= -2 \\\\\n(\\sqrt[3]{10 - 4x})^{3} &= (-2)^{3} \\\\\n10 - 4x &= -8 \\\\\n- 4x &= -18 \\\\\nx &= \\frac{9}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the root of $f(x)$ is\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac92\n$$\n\nA transformed radical function with an odd index will always have one real root." :tutorial/id #uuid "a49268e2-e58c-5966-ba98-b1786827ba76" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4864 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "ce36d68a-bbb4-56c0-b92d-852e3f103282" :step/math-academy-id 30460 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172880}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172880 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172023 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171829 :question/id #uuid "64957847-edce-52b1-9848-545c18014262" :question/math-academy-id "e-8143"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3e6159dd-c12e-5882-be17-815b4cc9ae52" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048784}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172609} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172610} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237896} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292700} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294898} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294899} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294900}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Roots of a Transformed Radical Function With an Odd Index"} :step/id #uuid "9a817a13-cf12-5a56-abd1-7fbe139fe7b4" :step/math-academy-id 30461 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172928}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172881 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172024 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171830 :question/id #uuid "96c06afd-4449-594e-8655-63f43ede736c" :question/math-academy-id "e-8144"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4d059c56-ae7f-5fb6-8bc6-2ce97bb7f876" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172607} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172608} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234483} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237900} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292704} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294901} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357543} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399357} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404544}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Roots of a Transformed Radical Function With an Even Index"} :step/id #uuid "9b6bd47e-dc8a-5923-bbcc-1f0390d2d1db" :step/math-academy-id 30463} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172928 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173170 :tutorial/content "Let's now learn how to find the root of a radical function with an even index. For example, let's try to find the root of\n\n$$\nf(x) = -3\\sqrt[4]{10x+1} + 9\n$$\n\nTo find the root of $f(x)$, we must solve\n\n$$\nf(x)=0\n$$\n\n$$\n-3\\sqrt[4]{10x+1} + 9 = 0\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation by making $x$ the subject, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-3\\sqrt[4]{10x + 1} + 9 &= 0 \\\\\n- 3\\sqrt[4]{10x + 1} &= -9 \\\\\n\\sqrt[4]{10x + 1} &= 3 \\\\\n(\\sqrt[4]{10x + 1})^{4} &= 3^{4} \\\\\n10x + 1 &= 81 \\\\\n10x &= 80 \\\\\nx &= 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we must check the answer by substituting it back into the initial equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-3\\sqrt[4]{10 \\cdot 8 + 1} + 9 &= 0 \\\\\n- 3\\sqrt[4]{81} + 9 &= 0 \\\\\n- 3 \\cdot 3 + 9 &= 0 \\\\\n0 &= 0✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the root of $f(x)$ is $x=8$.\n\nWhen the index of the radical function is even, we should always check for extraneous roots by substituting them back into the original equation.\n\nSometimes, a radical function with an even index has no root. Let's see an example." :tutorial/id #uuid "0a1feb60-62da-5279-8bfd-f8be6c389de7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5161 :tutorial/title "Finding the Roots of a Transformed Radical Function: Even Case"} :step/id #uuid "213cee54-dfad-5696-a210-97d588e61069" :step/math-academy-id 32450 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172881}}] :activity/title "Roots of Transformed Radical Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186172204}]}
